## Why This Is Asked
Tests NL2SQL grounding, RBAC enforcement, privacy controls, and explainability in a realistic Azure OpenAI setup.

## Key Concepts
- NL2SQL grounding to a versioned KB
- Tenant-scoped RBAC gating
- PII redaction in both prompts and results
- Explainable AI: SQL + execution plan
- Safe fallback when policies block access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch: authenticate, fetch KB, generate SQL, enforce guards
async function nl2sql(user, nlQuery) {
  const ctx = await authenticate(user);
  const kb = await fetchKB(ctx tenantId);
  const sql = await generateSQL(nlQuery, kb);
  const guarded = applyRBAC(sql, ctx);
  const redacted = redactPII(guarded, ctx);
  const explain = explainSQL(redacted);
  return { sql: redacted, explain };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you handle schema drift in the KB and keep responses in sync?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a privacy-preserving, multi-tenant AI workflow at scale, with strict data isolation, governance, and measurable latency.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant OpenAI deployment and prompt routing
- Real-time ingestion (Event Hubs) and low-latency scoring
- KB grounding via Cognitive Search on Blob data
- PII redaction (Text Analytics) and per-tenant masking (Key Vault)
- RBAC (Azure AD), Data Lake auditing, canary rollout

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: route prompt for tenant, redact, ground, respond
async function handleRequest(req){
  const tenantId = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const raw = req.body;
  const redacted = redactPII(raw);
  const kb = loadKBVersion(tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(redacted, kb, tenantId);
  const resp = await openai.complete(prompt, {deployment: getDeployment(tenantId)});
  logAudit(tenantId, prompt, resp);
  return resp;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to quantify latency vs. isolation trade-offs?
- How to implement drift detection for the KB and prompts across tenants?
